# 46. System Users

Linux also has accounts used by services.

Examples may include:

```text
www-data
nobody
daemon
messagebus
```

These accounts are often used so services do not have to run as root.

For example:

```text
Web Server
    ↓
www-data user
```

This helps reduce the impact of a compromised service.

---

# 47. Root vs Normal User

| Feature                           | Root                            | Normal User                 |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| UID                               | 0                               | Usually non-zero            |
| Administrative access             | Very high                       | Limited                     |
| Can manage users                  | Yes                             | Usually no                  |
| Can modify protected system files | Yes                             | Usually no                  |
| Home directory                    | `/root`                         | Usually `/home/username`    |
| Typical shell                     | `/bin/bash` or configured shell | Distribution/user dependent |
| Risk if compromised               | Very high                       | Usually more limited        |

---

# 48. Real-World Cybersecurity Example

Suppose an attacker compromises a normal web-server account.

If the web server runs as:

```text
root
```

the attacker may gain extremely powerful privileges.

But if it runs as:

```text
www-data
```

with restricted permissions, the attacker's initial access may be more limited.

This is an example of the **principle of least privilege**.

---

# 49. Principle of Least Privilege

The principle of least privilege means:

```text
Give a user or process only the permissions it actually needs.
```

For example:

A web server does not normally need unrestricted access to:

```text
/etc/shadow
/root
```

Therefore, it should run with a restricted service account whenever possible.

